#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# matroid_bases

#% text
# `matroid_bases(M)`: the bases of the matroid

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A matroid is `linear_matroid(A)` (the columns of a rational matrix), `graphic_matroid(edges)` (the edges of a graph), `uniform_matroid(r, m)` or `matroid(bases)` (bases as lists of elements 1, ..., m); its elements are numbered 1, ..., m, at most 22.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
matroid_bases(linear_matroid([[1, 0, 1, 2], [0, 1, 1, 2]]))
#% output
#= [[1, 2], [1, 3], [1, 4], [2, 3], [2, 4]]

#% cell
length(matroid_bases(graphic_matroid(subsets([1, 2, 3, 4], [2]))))
#% output
#= 16

